00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.530
- Yeah, so thank you for that question,

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:25.530
a lot of times when people
hear that I'm along with being

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:29.070
an educator, I'm in practice
and working primarily

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:31.710
with trauma survivors and
so people will often say,

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.176
oh, that must be depressing,

00:06:33.176 --> 00:06:36.840
but it actually is quite inspiring

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:41.190
because survivors are incredible people,

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:45.840
you know, they're, often, we wear it well

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.280
and people, if you just
encounter someone on the bus

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.410
or at work or in the
supermarket, you know,

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.510
you don't really know their story

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:58.170
but I get the opportunity
to be able to bear witness

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:01.580
and to hear people's stories and you know,

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:03.300
in that African-American culture

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:04.980
we would say kind of anyhow,

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:09.300
like what they have done anyhow is amazing

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.150
not only, you know, you think
about maybe professionally,

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:15.690
but personally to still
be a loving person,

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:20.407
to be a kind person, to
have grace is remarkable

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:24.870
given what some people have survived

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:29.870
and so seeing people's
courage and tenacity

00:07:30.192 --> 00:07:34.080
to try to live well is remarkable

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:37.440
and then I also do work
on the side of prevention,

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:40.290
I think if you're only
working in the aftermath,

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:42.810
it can feel like, you
know, what's the point?

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:44.700
There's always like gonna
be another school shooting

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:46.650
or another sexual assault or another child

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:50.370
who's been abused, but
when you get to also work

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:53.400
in helping stop the cycle,

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:56.070
so not only do I work with survivors,

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:59.910
but I have also worked with
persons who have offended

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.670
and so knowing that it's a choice

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:05.348
and that people can make another choice

00:08:05.348 --> 00:08:08.253
really helps me to keep showing up.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.670
- Wow, there's a lot there
I think on both sides,

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:13.710
even aspects of grace and
things I wanna definitely

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:16.440
come back to and kinda
peel back the layers on.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:17.580
Well, before we get there,

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.320
just also wondered if
you'd give our listeners

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.127
a little bit of a sense of
how you got into this work

00:08:22.127 --> 00:08:25.290
in the first place and how
you developed this passion

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:26.670
for the work that you do.

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:28.650
- Yes, I love to share it,

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:32.680
I am actually a pastor's daughter, and so

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:37.680
within the African American community,

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.900
a lot of times people
feel more comfortable

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.900
talking to their minister than a therapist

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.150
or psychologist or social worker

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:48.598
and so growing up I often saw people

00:08:48.598 --> 00:08:51.480
coming to meet really
with both of my parents

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:55.410
for what we would call pastoral
care or pastoral counseling,

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:56.730
in my book, "Homecoming,"

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.730
I say my teen years were
my first years working

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:02.444
at a crisis hotline
because people would just

00:09:02.444 --> 00:09:06.180
call the house at all hours of night

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.060
and day just with
whatever they were holding

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:14.060
and so I learned very early I would be

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:16.980
what people would describe as sensitive,

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:19.770
and you can either think
of like sensitive people

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:23.100
as that's a problem or that's a resource

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:28.050
or a strength that we feel
things deeply and you know,

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:33.050
really empathize with others
and have a love for listening

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:38.370
and for being of service and so, you know,

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:41.520
I was exposed to pastoral
counseling and then I discovered

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:43.440
like that it's a field in and of itself,

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.900
this is like something
where people, you know,

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:49.022
focus their life's work
and on human behavior

00:09:49.022 --> 00:09:52.974
and human interaction and
kind of working to the service

00:09:52.974 --> 00:09:57.780
to better society and humanity,

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:02.780
and so initially I thought I
wanted to practice full-time

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:06.300
and then when I applied to Duke University

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:10.830
where we both attended,
they really have a focus

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:12.516
on those who wanna do research

00:10:12.516 --> 00:10:15.600
and at first it wasn't a passion of mine,

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:17.754
but it's all about
getting the right exposure

00:10:17.754 --> 00:10:22.754
and I ended up working with
a faculty member, Susan Roth,

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:27.360
who works from the perspective
of feminist psychology,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.940
which means not just
looking at the individual

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:33.300
but paying attention to the
systems in which people live

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:37.170
and how to transform those
as well as individuals

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:39.570
and so that really got me excited

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:42.120
and also she does qualitative research,

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:45.840
which is documenting people's stories

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.840
and I love stories and I love storytelling

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:53.364
and so that got me interested in research

00:10:53.364 --> 00:10:55.113
and teaching as well.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:57.687
- Wow, that's wonderful
to hear about a journey

00:10:57.687 --> 00:10:59.520
and so many different pieces, and again,

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:01.930
and one of the themes I keep
hearing as you're talking about

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:04.170
just how you're doing day to day,

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:05.518
when you're talking about your passion,

00:11:05.518 --> 00:11:07.050
your educational experiences,

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:09.300
it's also just your
ability to kind of hold

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.952
different pieces together even
as you were thinking about

00:11:11.952 --> 00:11:14.820
the individual, also
thinking about the systems

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:16.350
as you were talking about
the work that you've done

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:17.700
with those who are survivors,

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.560
but then also working with offenders

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.750
and so I think there's a lot of strength

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:23.880
that likely comes from that as well

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:25.950
and then even as you talked
about being empathetic

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:29.340
and seeing that as a strength
and a way to kind of navigate

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:31.740
and help people through because
I think in so many instances

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:35.730
people do see that, but they
also see or feel the burden

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:38.280
because maybe things aren't
and there's never any sense

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.680
of true balance, but things
are too skewed on one side

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:43.140
or the other, so I'm just
curious as I'm kind of reflecting

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:44.730
on what you're saying, if that's something

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:46.500
that you've actively thought about

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.450
or if it's just something
that has kind of come natural

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:51.660
to you in terms of holding
multiple perspectives together

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:52.727
at once.

00:11:52.727 --> 00:11:55.620
- I love that and I
think it does capture it

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:57.210
that, you know, the way
I've thought about it

00:11:57.210 --> 00:12:01.560
is being holistic, you know,
so mind, body, heart, spirit,

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:06.560
and also like perspective
taking right to, you know,

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:09.007
when clients come in and tell their story

00:12:09.007 --> 00:12:11.790
or even sometimes I'm not
only working with individuals

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:13.465
but I work with couples and families

00:12:13.465 --> 00:12:17.250
and so it's important to be
able to see the circumstance

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:22.110
from different angles
because two people as we know

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:24.600
can have the quote unquote same experience

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:26.730
and see it totally differently,

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:30.330
so it helps to be able to
kinda step outside of yourself

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:34.680
and to be more whole, right?

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:39.680
To be grounded in the fact
that we're complicated,

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:41.820
people are complicated, right?

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:46.170
And so what we try to
simplify it or only see people

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:50.670
as one thing or based on
one moment in their life,

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:52.680
we're missing the full story.

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:55.710
So I do aim to be holistic,

00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:57.900
so my parents obviously
working in the church

00:12:57.900 --> 00:12:59.646
were very much into spirituality,

00:12:59.646 --> 00:13:02.910
but then they also were
very strong about education

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:05.690
and so it was important
for my brother and I

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:08.550
to really invest in our education

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:12.440
and then they taught us
that our culture was a gift,

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:17.440
so that, you know, just
being culturally rich

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:21.090
in high school, I was able
to move with my parents

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:23.220
to Liberia, West Africa,

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:27.750
so I think also living
outside of the states

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:32.640
gave me another vantage point
because I think sometimes

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:35.880
there can be a tendency
for us to center ourselves

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:39.176
as a nation and so to see like the world

00:13:39.176 --> 00:13:42.236
beyond Baltimore where I was growing up,

00:13:42.236 --> 00:13:47.236
really helped me to operate
I would say with a humility

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:51.137
and an appreciation for
the vast experiences

00:13:51.137 --> 00:13:53.130
that people have.

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:55.470
- Wow, that's really great

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:57.570
and just even the privilege
to be able to have those

00:13:57.570 --> 00:13:59.612
different experiences in different places,

00:13:59.612 --> 00:14:01.440
funny that you mentioned Liberia

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:03.600
'cause the guests that we
had on the last episode

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:05.460
actually grew up in Liberia

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:08.940
before and after the Civil
War and just talked about one,

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:11.430
just the strength that he had from being

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:13.740
in such a strong community before the war

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.960
and how that really
helped him stay grounded

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:17.070
also part of the faith community

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:18.990
even as he moved through the Civil War

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:21.240
but then coming here to the
states as you talked about,

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:24.180
different perspectives,
realizing that he was African,

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:26.310
but then also realizing
that he was African American

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:29.340
and a little bit of both and
trying to navigate through that

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:30.960
with all the things that have
happened here in the states,

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.450
so again, just such a rich perspective

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:35.640
that he has had and which
it sounds like you've had

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:36.810
in some different ways as well,

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:38.670
which I think is really, really helpful

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:40.320
and we can also do that when
we're here in the states,

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:43.050
I think sometimes we
forget about that as well

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:45.510
and just the importance
of just being exposed

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:46.830
to people who aren't like us

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:48.540
and how much we can really learn.

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:52.530
- It's so true and
actually the war in Liberia

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:55.980
is in part how I ended
up focusing on trauma.

00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:57.660
So we were there before the war

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:00.150
and then we ended up being evacuated

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:03.450
and it was that issue
of privilege as well,

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:06.720
you know, by being an American citizen

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:11.580
I was able to get on that
plane to go to safety

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:16.580
and so operating with that
awareness of, you know,

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:20.550
the impact of war, right?

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:25.550
Having loved ones who are lost
and just spaces and places,

00:15:26.676 --> 00:15:30.030
the ways in which war disrupts so much

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:33.090
and so then it's not just like a metaphor

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:35.520
or something far away
that you see on the news

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.802
but experiencing it up close in a place

00:15:37.802 --> 00:15:41.100
where there are not a lot
of licensed psychologists

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:43.680
and so, you know, then
asking that question of like,

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.692
how do we heal, right?

00:15:45.692 --> 00:15:50.692
So that is a part of how I
ended up focusing on trauma.

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:52.530
- That makes a lot of sense

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.480
and I think it's really
telling in terms of just

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:56.730
all the perspectives
that you brought as well

00:15:58.279 --> 00:16:00.210
and again, so many things
to kinda peel apart,

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:01.927
one question that I was just
gonna pull back to just,

00:16:01.927 --> 00:16:03.570
you know, as I'm thinking about

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:05.640
as many people are
listening to this episode,

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:07.350
I think a comment that
you made earlier about

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:10.140
people being complicated, also realizing

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:12.710
that everybody's perspective or experience

00:16:12.710 --> 00:16:14.910
in one situation can be so different,

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:17.100
I wonder, 'cause I'm imagining
some of our listeners

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:18.600
are hearing this, especially those

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:22.320
who maybe navigating with
people who are having

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.300
just challenges with everything going on

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:26.370
or even mental illness
and not always knowing

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:29.370
how to engage people, especially
if they feel like, okay,

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:30.420
well this is my loved one,

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:33.300
but I can clearly see that
the perspective is off

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:36.060
in this situation, how does
someone enter into that

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:38.670
without just saying, no, you're wrong,

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:40.740
while also trying to
understand and validate,

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:43.770
but then also kinda
help the person navigate

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:45.810
or get to a better place,
so anything you'd be willing

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:46.643
to share there?

00:16:46.643 --> 00:16:47.883
- Sure.

00:16:47.883 --> 00:16:52.883
So I think as you named first
being willing to listen,

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:57.810
I think often as soon as we
hear something we disagree with,

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:01.990
we jump in with a speech or with a debate

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:06.573
or with a correction and so
to start off with a curiosity

00:17:07.896 --> 00:17:11.250
of like, you know, so tell
me how you landed there.

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:15.690
Like how did you come to believe that

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:20.690
or to know that and is
there any part of you

00:17:20.820 --> 00:17:23.190
that feels torn about it?

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:28.190
Like is there any part of you
that you know, wonders about

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:32.940
is that the fullness of the story, right?

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:37.170
And so I think coming in with a curiosity

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:38.910
and a question is one piece,

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:43.590
I think another piece is
respecting our humanity

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:47.034
even when I don't have
respect for the opinion.

00:17:47.034 --> 00:17:50.690
Right, so I feel like the
opinion is very problematic

00:17:50.690 --> 00:17:53.512
and I have like no regard for that

00:17:53.512 --> 00:17:56.407
but you are a human being
sitting across from me,

00:17:56.407 --> 00:18:00.060
you are a member of my family
or whatever that connection

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:04.834
or relationship is and
so on that level alone,

00:18:04.834 --> 00:18:07.980
you know, by my own values,

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:11.310
there are certain ways in
which I will not speak to you

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:13.740
or there are certain
ways I won't treat you

00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:16.950
just as a result of honoring humanity.

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:20.250
And then I think it is also important

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:24.300
to give yourself permission
to speak the truth

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:27.510
as you know it because I, you know,

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:30.570
used to be one of those people
that would say, you know,

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:33.810
I'm not gonna say too
much to keep the peace

00:18:33.810 --> 00:18:35.520
and then I had to say to myself,

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:38.610
whose peace are you keeping, right?

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:39.750
So the people who were saying

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:42.840
all kinds of outrageous
things, they feel peaceful

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:45.330
'cause nobody is like challenging them

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:48.900
or asking them a question, and so to say,

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:53.310
it's not just for me
to be internally upset

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:55.410
while everybody else is comfortable,

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:58.440
but I also have a right to express myself

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:02.190
and my viewpoint and you know,

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:05.010
developing the capacity to do that even

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:07.560
when you're with people who don't agree

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:12.030
and that was a good learning
for me because you know,

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:15.840
there were times in my life
where I was primarily surrounded

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:17.580
by people who agree with me,

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:20.070
so then there are some
things you don't even learn

00:19:20.070 --> 00:19:23.130
how to articulate 'cause
everyone's always like,

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:25.378
yep, you know, you know, so we agree,

00:19:25.378 --> 00:19:29.278
so it was a muscle I had to develop,

00:19:29.278 --> 00:19:33.960
engaging with people who see
the world very differently

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:36.840
and learning how to, even if they're not

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:40.344
quote unquote convinced, but
at least being able to express,

00:19:40.344 --> 00:19:42.993
you know, what I believe and why.

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:46.530
- That's so good, I mean, I
think listeners hearing that

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:49.037
will take so many practical tips away.

00:19:49.037 --> 00:19:50.910
Even as you were talking I couldn't help

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:53.040
but not to politicize things
but I was thinking, wow,

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:55.008
if we could actually capture that,

00:19:55.008 --> 00:19:57.060
especially the respecting humanity,

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:59.580
if we could capture that as a nation,

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:02.208
we would be in a very different place.

00:20:02.208 --> 00:20:03.041
- Very different.

00:20:03.041 --> 00:20:07.890
Very different and it
requires that sense of safety,

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:11.250
and I think a lot of
people even at this moment

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:15.756
don't feel safe, you
know, feel like, you know,

00:20:15.756 --> 00:20:18.750
left to their own devices, other people

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:22.410
would either wipe me out
or wipe out my community

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:25.380
or would take away our rights
and all of these things,

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:28.290
so then when people are in a panic state

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:31.410
of feeling like that they are in danger,

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:35.162
then it's very hard to to
listen with compassion,

00:20:35.162 --> 00:20:38.334
so that's why I think
we need kind of those

00:20:38.334 --> 00:20:42.570
kind of commonalities and or ground rules

00:20:42.570 --> 00:20:46.020
of there are some things
we can disagree on

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:48.600
while still honoring
each other's humanity,

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:52.560
James Baldwin said
something to the effect of

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:56.670
we can agree to disagree
or we can be in community

00:20:56.670 --> 00:21:00.450
and disagree as long as our disagreement

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:04.620
is not a question of my
right to exist, right?

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:07.380
So that's that piece of,
there's a line, there is a line,

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:10.650
so yes we can have all kinds
of different viewpoints,

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:15.210
but when we start speaking
and moving in such a way

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:18.210
to deny people's right to exist,

00:21:18.210 --> 00:21:20.733
well then you're gonna
get a different response.

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:23.850
- That's so good, so worth repeating

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:27.000
in lots of different
circumstances and situations

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:29.403
so we can remind ourselves
about that enough.

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:33.120
And again, so much to
think about, you know,

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:35.670
just in terms of one-on-one
individual conversation,

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:38.370
but even in terms of
nationally and societally,

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:39.647
which I think is a good segue as well

00:21:39.647 --> 00:21:42.600
'cause I wondered if you give
people a little bit about

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:45.210
your journey to the APA and then also

00:21:45.210 --> 00:21:47.940
what the APA represents and does.

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:51.960
- Yes, so it's been a beautiful journey

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:55.800
for me within the American
Psychological Association

00:21:55.800 --> 00:21:59.100
and it starts with finding
like your common interest

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:00.483
or common community,

00:22:01.593 --> 00:22:03.240
the American Psychological Association

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:07.515
is a huge organization
over 130,000 members

00:22:07.515 --> 00:22:11.186
and so when we think about
notions such as belonging

00:22:11.186 --> 00:22:14.970
or organizational home, for many of us,

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:16.770
you find a home within the home

00:22:16.770 --> 00:22:19.560
and so we have different divisions

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:22.860
and one of the divisions is
a society for the psychology

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:23.850
of women.

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:28.470
And so that was kind of
my first home within APA

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:32.130
and connecting with
people who are committed

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:34.620
to bettering the lives of women and girls

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:37.230
and addressing our mental health issues

00:22:37.230 --> 00:22:41.918
and so at the time, soon after I graduated

00:22:41.918 --> 00:22:44.850
from graduate school, the president

00:22:44.850 --> 00:22:48.810
for that women's division
was Jessica Henderson Daniel,

00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:52.980
and she is a Black woman
who went on to become

00:22:52.980 --> 00:22:56.010
APA'S first Black woman president.

00:22:56.010 --> 00:22:58.380
So then it is not by accident, right,

00:22:58.380 --> 00:23:02.010
that I am in this seat
now, mentorship matters

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:07.010
and she, you know, when I
graduated with my doctorate I was,

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:10.590
you know, in my early
twenties and she, you know,

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:13.890
took me under her wing,
it was a group of us

00:23:13.890 --> 00:23:16.380
'cause one of her
initiatives was mentorship.

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:20.280
And so she had us all kind of
articulate professional goals

00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:23.010
and then there were a group
of mentors to like help us

00:23:23.010 --> 00:23:25.020
to navigate those spaces.

00:23:25.020 --> 00:23:29.460
So, along with having a passion
for women's mental health,

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:32.490
I have a big interest in global issues

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:36.210
and so soon after I graduated
the American Psychological

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:41.190
Association, received
non-governmental organizational status

00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:45.000
at the United Nations and
so they put out a call

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:47.153
that they were gonna
have six representatives

00:23:47.153 --> 00:23:49.830
and I had just graduated, so I said, oh,

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:51.570
they're probably not gonna pick me,

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:54.660
but I'm gonna apply anyway
and this is an important point

00:23:54.660 --> 00:23:57.019
for all of our students
or early career people

00:23:57.019 --> 00:24:00.630
or even mid-career, don't
count yourself out, right?

00:24:00.630 --> 00:24:02.250
Sometimes we eliminate ourselves,

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:03.900
it's like make them tell you no

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:06.300
instead of you telling yourself no

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:08.445
and so I was one of the applicants,

00:24:08.445 --> 00:24:11.177
I got called into New
York for the interview,

00:24:11.177 --> 00:24:14.250
they were picking, I
believe there were six of us

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:19.250
for that initial assignment
and at the interview

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:21.150
I was the only one in the interview

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:24.270
who mentioned racism and coming up

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:26.910
was the World Conference Against Racism,

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:29.250
so they needed one of
the six representatives

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:33.627
to cover that convention, and
so I ended up being selected

00:24:33.627 --> 00:24:38.627
and so that was a big
part of my work in APA

00:24:40.802 --> 00:24:45.802
and then working as a
leader and a researcher

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:52.190
in my work around trauma really
added to the contributions

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:55.830
around trauma psychology and so some,

00:24:55.830 --> 00:25:00.830
maybe 10 years after that,
I had told my mentor,

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:06.540
the women's division is
division 35 and I said,

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:09.640
I wanna be president of
division 35 before I'm 35

00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:13.874
and so I was like, you know that,

00:25:13.874 --> 00:25:15.450
and so she said, are you willing to work?

00:25:15.450 --> 00:25:16.350
And I said, yes.

00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:18.280
She said, okay, we're
gonna make it happen,

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:23.079
so at 34 I was elected,
president they had had,

00:25:23.079 --> 00:25:27.180
and it's funny how your
different training serves you,

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:28.500
when we had our first meeting,

00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:30.330
they were all shocked and were like,

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:31.830
how'd you learn how to run a meeting?

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:34.320
They thought they were gonna
have to like handle things

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:36.240
but growing up in the black church,

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:38.100
there's a young people's division

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:39.570
and you run your own meetings

00:25:39.570 --> 00:25:41.130
with "Robert's Rules of Order,"

00:25:41.130 --> 00:25:44.815
like you know how to call for
a vote and all these things,

00:25:44.815 --> 00:25:45.855
I was like, oh I've been doing that--

00:25:45.855 --> 00:25:48.022
- You're seasoned already.

00:25:49.355 --> 00:25:53.580
- So, it was a beautiful year,

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:57.570
well you operate as a
trio and then after that,

00:25:57.570 --> 00:26:00.840
people kept planting the
seed of you should run

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:02.970
for president of the whole thing.

00:26:02.970 --> 00:26:06.120
And initially it was
not an interest of mine

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:08.910
'cause I had my mind on I
wanted to do the women's group,

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:11.430
I did it, I'm kinda
finished and gone back to

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:15.780
my academic work and my private practice,

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:19.740
but you know, timing is
interesting and important,

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:23.490
so when people circled around
the most recent time and said,

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:25.290
there's a group of us
who want to support you,

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:26.907
are you willing to run?

00:26:26.907 --> 00:26:29.760
And for the first time I thought about it

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:33.630
and especially as a trauma
psychologist, I was seeing both,

00:26:33.630 --> 00:26:37.650
we were in the pandemic in
the realities of racial trauma

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:40.788
and I felt like this is the time.

00:26:40.788 --> 00:26:43.320
So I entered the race,

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:46.830
I will say financially it
was a great year to run

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:49.020
because all of the campaign was online,

00:26:49.020 --> 00:26:50.670
so you just send me the Zoom link

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:54.783
instead of having to like
fly all over the country

00:26:54.783 --> 00:26:57.390
and you know, do all of that,

00:26:57.390 --> 00:27:02.070
so virtual elections kind
of level the playing field

00:27:02.070 --> 00:27:02.903
to some extent.

00:27:03.901 --> 00:27:08.567
So I am really grateful and excited

00:27:08.567 --> 00:27:12.420
about the work we've
been doing, so this 2023

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:15.600
is my presidential year,
but I remain on the board

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:19.620
for three years and my focus
areas are trauma, grief,

00:27:19.620 --> 00:27:24.460
and oppression and just doing
some really exciting things.

00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:25.980
- Yeah, that's wonderful to hear.

00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:28.302
I mean, just the whole
navigation and story of it.

00:27:28.302 --> 00:27:31.617
So, I mean, it sounds like
the need and your passion

00:27:31.617 --> 00:27:34.045
met together and that's what shifted

00:27:34.045 --> 00:27:37.560
your narrative in terms of
your willingness to jump in.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:42.069
- Yes, of seeing this is
like, and years before me,

00:27:42.069 --> 00:27:45.060
a lot of presidents
would make the argument

00:27:45.060 --> 00:27:48.340
of how they could convince society

00:27:51.413 --> 00:27:52.677
that psychology had something to offer

00:27:52.677 --> 00:27:53.678
but the time that we were living in,

00:27:53.678 --> 00:27:55.470
it's like all the
therapists I knew were full,

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:58.290
so it wasn't just a matter of
like trying to convince them

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:00.870
we have something to
offer is more the nation

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:03.720
and even the world or turning
to people of our profession

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:06.000
and saying, what can we do, right?

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:10.173
People are overwhelmed, the
grief, the stress, the loss,

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:16.100
and so it was just a
very different climate

00:28:16.170 --> 00:28:19.330
where you're being
called on as a profession

00:28:19.330 --> 00:28:24.330
to really fill a void
that as in human terms,

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:28.470
we'd never experienced
anything like this before,

00:28:28.470 --> 00:28:32.430
like the numbers of losses, you know,

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:36.933
it was it is really life-changing.

00:28:38.520 --> 00:28:39.897
- Yeah, and so important in so many ways

00:28:39.897 --> 00:28:42.150
and I mean I can just hear
the passion in your voice

00:28:42.150 --> 00:28:44.430
for all those pieces as well

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:46.020
and just, I mean, as you mentioned,

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:47.520
just it seems like there's
some gratitude also

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:52.520
about how people have now
caught the need and the vision.

00:28:52.980 --> 00:28:55.870
But at the same time it
seems at least in my option,

00:28:55.870 --> 00:28:57.210
there's still a tension there as well

00:28:57.210 --> 00:28:58.860
because there's such a huge need,

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:01.140
so I'm also curious, and I'm
sure our listeners are curious

00:29:01.140 --> 00:29:03.660
about you as an organization,

00:29:03.660 --> 00:29:06.570
how do you address such a large need

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:08.910
where a lot of people are
saying this is invaluable

00:29:08.910 --> 00:29:11.280
and others are saying there
aren't enough psychologists

00:29:11.280 --> 00:29:12.780
especially if you think about,

00:29:13.824 --> 00:29:15.162
I mean, just in society, on high schools,

00:29:15.162 --> 00:29:16.750
on college campuses, how are you all

00:29:16.750 --> 00:29:18.633
trying to navigate that?

00:29:19.590 --> 00:29:23.580
- One of the things is trying
to increase the numbers

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:28.214
in the field and it is excited to report

00:29:28.214 --> 00:29:31.290
out of the advanced placement courses,

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:33.510
the AP courses in high school,

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:38.220
we have AP psychology
classes and AP psychology

00:29:38.220 --> 00:29:42.630
is right now the number one
choice among the sciences,

00:29:42.630 --> 00:29:45.660
that so many students are
interested in selecting it,

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:48.000
they see how it connects to their lives

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:52.260
and so in that, working with educators

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:54.120
within the school system,
'cause one of the things

00:29:54.120 --> 00:29:56.700
we know is people can't pick a career

00:29:56.700 --> 00:29:58.620
if they never heard of it, right?

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:01.230
So it's like they heard of
like doctor, nurse, teacher,

00:30:01.230 --> 00:30:05.280
firefighter, and so, raising the awareness

00:30:05.280 --> 00:30:08.760
not only about the field of
psychology that it exists,

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:11.940
but also the range of things
that you can do with it

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:13.560
because, you know, from your own work,

00:30:13.560 --> 00:30:17.430
knowing it's not only about
those who are in practice,

00:30:17.430 --> 00:30:22.290
but the rich scientific work
that's being done, education,

00:30:22.290 --> 00:30:24.630
and then we have applied psychology

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:27.720
or industrial organizational psychology

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:29.603
so, people who work, you know,

00:30:29.603 --> 00:30:31.983
we talk about transforming systems.

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:35.880
and so you know, we at Pepperdine,

00:30:35.880 --> 00:30:37.290
along with our doctoral program,

00:30:37.290 --> 00:30:40.890
we have a master's program
and I tell our students,

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:43.331
whatever you ultimately choose to do,

00:30:43.331 --> 00:30:45.270
this degree will serve you

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:47.850
because it's gonna involve people, right?

00:30:47.850 --> 00:30:51.000
So even people end up going
into law or to business,

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:54.810
having a fundamental
understanding about psychology

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:56.250
can help them.

00:30:56.250 --> 00:30:58.830
So, that's a part of the
work that we're doing

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:02.520
is trying to increase the field

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:06.780
and also trying to provide advocacy

00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:09.450
using psychological science.

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:13.500
And so there are issues that
people are advocating for,

00:31:13.500 --> 00:31:16.830
whether we're talking
about climate change,

00:31:16.830 --> 00:31:19.225
whether we're talking
about access to healthcare,

00:31:19.225 --> 00:31:22.890
but being able to document which we can,

00:31:22.890 --> 00:31:26.040
the psychological costs, consequences,

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:30.274
and even some solutions,
I think helps to inform

00:31:30.274 --> 00:31:32.835
our policy makers, our legislators,

00:31:32.835 --> 00:31:36.480
and so we just on this past Monday,

00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:39.810
had one of our advocacy
days where our members

00:31:39.810 --> 00:31:42.840
set up virtual meetings with senators

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:46.440
and with congressional
representatives about, you know,

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:49.200
some of the key issues in educating them,

00:31:49.200 --> 00:31:52.710
we also provide testimony
on the hill about, you know,

00:31:52.710 --> 00:31:54.720
whatever the issue, school shootings,

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:56.426
whatever it is people are facing,

00:31:56.426 --> 00:32:00.060
what we can contribute to the conversation

00:32:00.060 --> 00:32:02.430
so that we can come up with some solutions

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:04.173
that really have impact.

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:06.420
- I mean, that's encouraging

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:07.830
to hear how it's really cutting across

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:11.010
so many different domains and
systems, and as you mentioned,

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:13.290
because that psychology
is gonna be so important

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:15.360
in so many different ways,
I mean, a little bit,

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:18.300
maybe selfishly, just as I've
noticed with a neuroscience

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:19.920
and someone who does a lot
of behavioral neuroscience,

00:32:19.920 --> 00:32:21.180
I've talked to university presidents

00:32:21.180 --> 00:32:22.534
who are behavioral neuroscientists

00:32:22.534 --> 00:32:25.650
and it's amazing seeing how
they navigate with people

00:32:25.650 --> 00:32:28.770
just based on the way that
they watch people's behaviors

00:32:28.770 --> 00:32:30.642
and how that has really helped them

00:32:30.642 --> 00:32:32.700
in their leadership roles,

00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:34.410
so I think what you've
mentioned to your students

00:32:34.410 --> 00:32:35.970
is so important.

00:32:35.970 --> 00:32:38.670
Even as you're talking about
policy, I mean, a lot of times,

00:32:38.670 --> 00:32:40.710
probably the last 10 to 15
years we've talked a lot

00:32:40.710 --> 00:32:42.930
in this country about needing to increase

00:32:42.930 --> 00:32:45.480
our scientific literacy across society

00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:48.540
but I think, if anything,
the pandemic has shown us

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:50.033
how important that is in terms of

00:32:50.033 --> 00:32:52.107
our psychological understanding

00:32:52.107 --> 00:32:54.060
and how much differently we would navigate

00:32:54.060 --> 00:32:57.030
if we had that understanding
in the broader perspective.

00:32:57.030 --> 00:32:59.730
- And how that even became politicized,

00:32:59.730 --> 00:33:02.370
like these attacks on science

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:04.680
and so it was like, for some scientists,

00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:07.890
it was their first time
ever for participating

00:33:07.890 --> 00:33:10.110
in a protest march, it's like, you know,

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:12.780
people were thinking they picked a career

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:15.180
that was quote unquote
apolitical but, you know,

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:17.835
right, somehow, here we,

00:33:17.835 --> 00:33:22.380
so, yeah, it's so important
and then when you brought up

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:24.540
this issue of leadership, you know,

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:28.920
with our researchers
find about the necessity

00:33:28.920 --> 00:33:31.233
and the value of being
relational, you know,

00:33:32.155 --> 00:33:33.530
whether we talk about
emotional intelligence

00:33:33.530 --> 00:33:36.780
or social skills, however we name that,

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:39.360
that it's not just about the outcome,

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:42.177
meaning the labor or
like what are the goals

00:33:42.177 --> 00:33:43.980
but as my mother would say,

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:46.761
it does matter how you treat people that,

00:33:46.761 --> 00:33:50.730
so some of these wisdoms
that we find our grandmothers

00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:53.340
or our culture could have taught us

00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:58.020
but to be able to document
it, I think is so important

00:33:58.020 --> 00:34:01.170
as well in terms of what
helps when you're trying to,

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:04.610
is it motivate people, mobilize people

00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:07.770
and for sustainable change.

00:34:07.770 --> 00:34:10.410
- Yeah, well, again,
I was already grateful

00:34:10.410 --> 00:34:11.640
that you're in your role as president,

00:34:11.640 --> 00:34:13.800
but feeling even more
grateful for the work

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:15.690
that you're doing and
I'm sure working in teams

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:17.790
to kinda move a lot of
these things forward

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:19.170
across society as well.

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:20.940
- Oh, thank you so much.

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:23.880
I am so excited, and you know,

00:34:23.880 --> 00:34:26.340
one of the things I've
talked about or described

00:34:26.340 --> 00:34:30.150
is as a movement in the sense
that many people are involved

00:34:30.150 --> 00:34:33.330
and I tell people like 2023 is
a blip on the screen, right?

00:34:33.330 --> 00:34:34.860
I get to president for this year

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:36.306
and then it's not gonna be my year anymore

00:34:36.306 --> 00:34:40.290
but what encourages me
is there are so many

00:34:40.290 --> 00:34:43.754
like-minded people in
various roles and positions,

00:34:43.754 --> 00:34:48.450
within APA, outside of
APA, and so when we're all,

00:34:48.450 --> 00:34:51.090
you know, moving at the same time

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:53.823
then we can really see
some great things happen.

00:34:54.969 --> 00:34:56.670
- Yeah, really, really well said.

00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:57.990
So I know we've also talked, you know,

00:34:57.990 --> 00:35:00.150
speaking of relationships
and a relational things

00:35:00.150 --> 00:35:01.110
and thinking about community,

00:35:01.110 --> 00:35:03.480
we've also talked quite
a bit about aspects

00:35:03.480 --> 00:35:04.800
of your faith growing up

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:07.140
and so I was curious if
you could bring that back

00:35:07.140 --> 00:35:11.280
into the conversation, both
in terms of how as a field,

00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:14.342
and I'm not speculating, but giving you

00:35:14.342 --> 00:35:17.190
some of my observations,
it seems that psychology

00:35:17.190 --> 00:35:20.010
has been more accepting
of the fact that faith

00:35:20.010 --> 00:35:21.930
is a large part of people's lives,

00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:23.220
so if you'd wanna comment on that,

00:35:23.220 --> 00:35:26.460
and then also how that for you
is an integrated experience

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:28.533
as a psychologist and as a leader.

00:35:29.880 --> 00:35:31.920
- Absolutely, I'm so excited to

00:35:31.920 --> 00:35:36.110
be one of the many people who
is doing bridge building work

00:35:36.110 --> 00:35:41.110
in this area that when the
field of psychology started,

00:35:41.130 --> 00:35:43.470
you know, many of the
pioneers in the field

00:35:43.470 --> 00:35:46.440
were people of faith, it
wasn't seen as attention

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:51.153
and psychology, you know,
literally means study of the soul,

00:35:51.990 --> 00:35:54.240
but then as the field developed,

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:58.950
I think in some ways trying to
establish itself as a science

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:02.820
led some leaders in the
field to like disconnect

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:06.270
from spirituality and it can also be

00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:08.380
like from their own experiences

00:36:10.473 --> 00:36:12.780
when some people overgeneralize

00:36:12.780 --> 00:36:16.830
and only see that faith-based
space as a place of shame

00:36:16.830 --> 00:36:21.830
or a place of harm and what
I do is I hold both truths

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:24.663
and I encourage our students
to hold both truths,

00:36:26.387 --> 00:36:27.966
which is there are people for whom

00:36:27.966 --> 00:36:30.030
it has been a life-saving resource,

00:36:30.030 --> 00:36:33.330
and that it's been healing
and nourishing and edifying,

00:36:33.330 --> 00:36:37.140
and there are people who have
been harmed in those spaces.

00:36:37.140 --> 00:36:40.740
And so, both things are true,
I think what ends up happening

00:36:40.740 --> 00:36:42.990
is people go from one
extreme to the other,

00:36:42.990 --> 00:36:45.300
so either if you experience it as harmful,

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:47.640
it's hard to see anything good about it,

00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:52.290
or people who love it don't
believe any of the reports

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:54.960
that have come forward or
people who have been harmed

00:36:54.960 --> 00:36:58.050
and so we, you know, wanna
be really careful about that

00:36:58.050 --> 00:37:02.160
and recognize we never wanna put people

00:37:02.160 --> 00:37:05.910
in a position where they feel
they have to choose, right?

00:37:05.910 --> 00:37:10.680
So if psychologists say dismissive
things about your faith,

00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:13.860
like I literally had a
client who shared with me

00:37:13.860 --> 00:37:18.213
that their former therapist
was like very warm

00:37:18.213 --> 00:37:20.520
until the client shared they
were in seminary training

00:37:20.520 --> 00:37:24.840
to be a minister and then
the therapist responded,

00:37:24.840 --> 00:37:28.461
you seem so smart, don't tell
me you believe that stuff.

00:37:28.461 --> 00:37:30.570
Like what in the world?

00:37:30.570 --> 00:37:33.499
Like what an attack on the
very core of this person,

00:37:33.499 --> 00:37:37.080
so, there have been
statements like that directly

00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:40.470
or indirectly that have been
made by people in the field

00:37:40.470 --> 00:37:41.820
and then on the other side,

00:37:41.820 --> 00:37:44.430
I have heard from pulpits and you know,

00:37:44.430 --> 00:37:48.780
sermons where people will
say things like, you know,

00:37:48.780 --> 00:37:51.300
you don't need therapy, you need to pray

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:54.090
and presenting this
false choice, you know,

00:37:54.090 --> 00:37:58.140
I had a client who her
pastor literally told her,

00:37:58.140 --> 00:38:00.540
you need to stop letting
those devil people

00:38:00.540 --> 00:38:04.050
mess with your mind, I
already prayed for you.

00:38:04.050 --> 00:38:09.050
And so, to stop creating attention,

00:38:10.500 --> 00:38:14.910
but instead to recognize
that collectively,

00:38:14.910 --> 00:38:18.690
we can provide the support
that people are so much

00:38:18.690 --> 00:38:22.470
in need of, you know, I
have so much respect for

00:38:22.470 --> 00:38:23.910
a lot of times faith communities

00:38:23.910 --> 00:38:27.060
are like people's chosen home or family

00:38:27.060 --> 00:38:30.000
in the sense that a lot,
especially black churches

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:32.100
will have programs all week long, right.

00:38:32.100 --> 00:38:33.990
So as a therapist, I might, you know,

00:38:33.990 --> 00:38:35.820
we might have one hour a week, right,

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:40.820
with 50 minutes an hour but
then the rest of the week

00:38:40.890 --> 00:38:43.080
people may be like on their own,

00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:45.960
so they may go like Monday
for choir rehearsal,

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:49.440
Tuesday, Bible study, Wednesday
they're helping to feed

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:52.950
the homeless and that is their community.

00:38:52.950 --> 00:38:56.880
So there are things that we both give

00:38:56.880 --> 00:38:59.850
and I think it just requires
greater understanding

00:38:59.850 --> 00:39:02.515
of each other and respect.

00:39:02.515 --> 00:39:04.230
- Yeah, it's so well said and I mean,

00:39:04.230 --> 00:39:06.660
all those stories that you shared, I mean,

00:39:06.660 --> 00:39:08.910
I think sometimes to your
point about people having

00:39:08.910 --> 00:39:10.110
one perspective about faith,

00:39:10.110 --> 00:39:13.080
either for or against based
on their personal experience,

00:39:13.080 --> 00:39:15.180
then also it's sometimes hard to, again,

00:39:15.180 --> 00:39:17.170
as you mentioned earlier,

00:39:17.170 --> 00:39:18.540
think about an experience
outside of your own

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:20.820
and how important it is to
have that understanding,

00:39:20.820 --> 00:39:24.180
but how much those comments
still happen on both sides

00:39:24.180 --> 00:39:25.410
'cause I see them all the time as well,

00:39:25.410 --> 00:39:28.050
whether I'm in the classroom or in the lab

00:39:28.050 --> 00:39:29.993
or in the community, and just making sure

00:39:29.993 --> 00:39:31.770
that people actually have an understanding

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:34.050
of what's happening in each space

00:39:34.050 --> 00:39:35.700
and it's been encouraging, you know,

00:39:35.700 --> 00:39:37.810
to see the way some of those

00:39:37.810 --> 00:39:39.210
have turned into collaborations as well

00:39:39.210 --> 00:39:41.070
which is really healthy.

00:39:41.070 --> 00:39:44.220
- That's, you know, what's
been beautiful is, you know,

00:39:44.220 --> 00:39:47.280
at my church in South Los Angeles

00:39:47.280 --> 00:39:50.610
doing mental health
ministry, so, you know,

00:39:50.610 --> 00:39:55.410
literally we'll do workshops
on dealing with holiday stress

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:59.520
or grief and loss, healthy
versus unhealthy relationships,

00:39:59.520 --> 00:40:02.700
and so people, you know,
show up and they, you know,

00:40:02.700 --> 00:40:04.818
want the knowledge, they
want the information,

00:40:04.818 --> 00:40:07.085
and to have it in that
space lets them know

00:40:07.085 --> 00:40:09.030
that it's okay to make use of it, right,

00:40:09.030 --> 00:40:12.138
for it to be accessible.

00:40:12.138 --> 00:40:15.240
I remember at one of my former churches,

00:40:15.240 --> 00:40:20.240
the pastor talked openly in
her sermons about having gone

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:23.820
to therapy and it just gave
so many people permission

00:40:23.820 --> 00:40:26.417
to feel like, like we
know she's holy, right?

00:40:26.417 --> 00:40:29.190
We know like she prays all the time,

00:40:29.190 --> 00:40:32.310
so if she could do, you
know, make use of it

00:40:32.310 --> 00:40:34.483
then like, maybe it could help me too.

00:40:34.483 --> 00:40:38.160
So I think, you know, it
can start from the top

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:42.120
in terms of leadership giving
more room and permission

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:45.680
and encouragement for people
to make use of the services.

00:40:45.680 --> 00:40:48.600
- Yeah, that's really
true and really healthy.

00:40:48.600 --> 00:40:51.870
Do you see any roles from
an organizational standpoint

00:40:51.870 --> 00:40:55.620
for a group like the APA in
some of these conversations?

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:58.210
And if so, like what does
that look like exactly?

00:40:58.210 --> 00:41:01.380
- Yes, so as I was mentioning,

00:41:01.380 --> 00:41:04.204
we have multiple divisions
and even one of our divisions

00:41:04.204 --> 00:41:08.026
has a focus on faith,
spirituality, and religion,

00:41:08.026 --> 00:41:13.026
and so it covers all the
different domains of our work,

00:41:14.220 --> 00:41:17.580
so in terms of research, it is important

00:41:17.580 --> 00:41:21.370
to be able to explore both
quantitatively and qualitatively,

00:41:21.370 --> 00:41:26.100
you know, what is the impact of faith

00:41:26.100 --> 00:41:29.516
or being a part of a faith
community or faith practices,

00:41:29.516 --> 00:41:32.430
what are things that
are helpful or harmful?

00:41:32.430 --> 00:41:34.470
So, you know, there's
something called positive

00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:37.170
religious coping or
negative religious coping,

00:41:37.170 --> 00:41:40.586
so how you picture God or think about God

00:41:40.586 --> 00:41:44.460
is gonna affect whether or not your faith

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:48.030
is de-stressing for you or
encouraging for you, right?

00:41:48.030 --> 00:41:49.890
If you're thinking like,
there is this figure

00:41:49.890 --> 00:41:52.110
that's out to get me and who's mad at me

00:41:52.110 --> 00:41:53.910
and hear all these things, then yeah,

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:56.040
I'm gonna be stressed out
and then it's gonna be

00:41:56.040 --> 00:41:57.690
a negative thing in my life,

00:41:57.690 --> 00:42:00.540
versus, do I really
picture that as some source

00:42:00.540 --> 00:42:04.656
that is loving, that cares about
me, that wants good for me,

00:42:04.656 --> 00:42:08.580
then I'm gonna have a different
psychological experience

00:42:08.580 --> 00:42:11.343
as I think about my
relationship with that.

00:42:12.690 --> 00:42:15.540
And even for those who
may not be religious

00:42:15.540 --> 00:42:17.670
in the sense of like belief in a God,

00:42:17.670 --> 00:42:20.280
but as spiritual beings, you know,

00:42:20.280 --> 00:42:24.150
what are those principles
that guide their lives?

00:42:24.150 --> 00:42:27.073
Because I say wherever you
have human beings involved,

00:42:27.073 --> 00:42:32.073
you're gonna have blessing
and difficulty, right,

00:42:32.460 --> 00:42:35.280
so even some people who
will take the God part out,

00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:37.440
but you have people
gathering for a book club

00:42:37.440 --> 00:42:39.810
or gathering for yoga,
whatever you have people

00:42:39.810 --> 00:42:42.837
gathering for, they're gonna
bring their stuff into it

00:42:42.837 --> 00:42:44.640
and some of that will be edifying

00:42:44.640 --> 00:42:46.912
and some of it will be destructive.

00:42:46.912 --> 00:42:51.308
So yeah, we just wanna give
space for the truth of,

00:42:51.308 --> 00:42:55.350
you know, we are works in progress

00:42:55.350 --> 00:43:00.350
and to be able to see the
ideals or the principles

00:43:01.221 --> 00:43:06.221
as opposed to people's
imperfect variations

00:43:06.870 --> 00:43:08.463
of trying to live that out.

00:43:09.330 --> 00:43:10.530
- Yeah, that's so well said,

00:43:10.530 --> 00:43:12.870
reminds me of something you
said even before we started

00:43:12.870 --> 00:43:14.820
this recording, just in
terms of also bringing

00:43:14.820 --> 00:43:17.307
your whole authentic self
to this work as well,

00:43:17.307 --> 00:43:19.260
and so I know you're talking
from a leadership position,

00:43:19.260 --> 00:43:22.110
but it seems like there's also
aspects of leading by example

00:43:22.110 --> 00:43:23.790
in your own life as well.

00:43:23.790 --> 00:43:27.049
- Right, and that authentic truth-telling

00:43:27.049 --> 00:43:31.380
to not have to pretend
to have all the answers,

00:43:31.380 --> 00:43:33.900
or I think this is a big
one with faith leaders,

00:43:33.900 --> 00:43:37.590
when people are in grief or trauma,

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:39.720
they're often asking us like, why?

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:41.400
Like, why did this happen?

00:43:41.400 --> 00:43:44.340
And because there's not a
cookie cutter easy answer,

00:43:44.340 --> 00:43:46.890
sometimes people have said
things that are harmful

00:43:46.890 --> 00:43:48.870
just trying to create an answer

00:43:48.870 --> 00:43:51.810
and so even for us to
give room for the mystery

00:43:51.810 --> 00:43:54.630
to be able to say, I'm
not sure like, you know,

00:43:54.630 --> 00:43:59.630
why your mom died so young
or, you know, and yet right,

00:44:00.190 --> 00:44:04.710
we're here to be with each
other, to support each other,

00:44:04.710 --> 00:44:09.710
so I think the humility and the honesty

00:44:11.220 --> 00:44:16.220
really does much more
than trying to dictate

00:44:16.860 --> 00:44:18.930
some fake answer.

00:44:18.930 --> 00:44:20.670
- Yeah, yeah, it's a healthy place,

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:23.109
but not always an easy place to come to.

00:44:23.109 --> 00:44:24.600
- Right, yep.

00:44:24.600 --> 00:44:25.890
- So appreciate the way that, again,

00:44:25.890 --> 00:44:28.530
you're leading by example and
then also just putting things

00:44:28.530 --> 00:44:30.420
in place and having these
conversations and policies

00:44:30.420 --> 00:44:33.637
to really help us get to that
place as a society as well.

00:44:33.637 --> 00:44:35.280
- And mentioning policy,
when you asked about,

00:44:35.280 --> 00:44:37.053
you know, things that APA is doing,

00:44:37.053 --> 00:44:40.790
there are a group of psychologists now

00:44:40.790 --> 00:44:44.640
that are working for some
guidelines to help people

00:44:44.640 --> 00:44:48.150
who are in practice to know
how to integrate religion

00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:51.161
and spirituality respectfully,
but appropriately,

00:44:51.161 --> 00:44:54.060
so when we think about
like cultural awareness,

00:44:54.060 --> 00:44:56.190
people often think about
race and ethnicity,

00:44:56.190 --> 00:44:59.790
maybe they think about gender
sexuality or disability,

00:44:59.790 --> 00:45:01.884
but religion often gets
left out and you know,

00:45:01.884 --> 00:45:04.920
our researchers find in general,

00:45:04.920 --> 00:45:09.150
the general public endorses
higher levels of religiosity

00:45:09.150 --> 00:45:10.579
than mental health providers.

00:45:10.579 --> 00:45:15.092
So we don't wanna leave it
out and want to help equip

00:45:15.092 --> 00:45:18.360
those who are of service to others to know

00:45:18.360 --> 00:45:21.450
how to integrate it appropriately.

00:45:21.450 --> 00:45:23.550
- Yeah, that's so well said

00:45:23.550 --> 00:45:26.280
and encouraging to know that
it's also based on the research

00:45:26.280 --> 00:45:28.740
and what you're seeing as well
and then just anecdotally,

00:45:28.740 --> 00:45:30.840
I've definitely seen that
in terms of the circles

00:45:30.840 --> 00:45:33.270
that I've moved in, whether
that's in psychology circles

00:45:33.270 --> 00:45:34.650
or even in scientific circles

00:45:34.650 --> 00:45:37.133
at people who have been having
these diversity, equity,

00:45:38.173 --> 00:45:39.300
inclusion conversations and realizing

00:45:39.300 --> 00:45:42.510
that the faith component has
oftentimes been left out,

00:45:42.510 --> 00:45:44.040
but I think especially the last few years,

00:45:44.040 --> 00:45:47.262
realizing how harmful
that can be to pretend

00:45:47.262 --> 00:45:48.930
that it doesn't exist, so.

00:45:48.930 --> 00:45:51.420
- That's right, yeah, it's there

00:45:51.420 --> 00:45:54.360
and then we can do harm
out of ignorance, you know,

00:45:54.360 --> 00:45:58.772
like we have a clinic where
we provide community care

00:45:58.772 --> 00:46:02.190
for like a lower rate and you know,

00:46:02.190 --> 00:46:04.890
one of the students came to
me because she had a client

00:46:04.890 --> 00:46:07.450
who was fasting and the
student was not familiar

00:46:09.061 --> 00:46:10.410
with fasting, so, if you
don't know what that is,

00:46:10.410 --> 00:46:12.150
sure you can, create in your minds

00:46:12.150 --> 00:46:15.270
all kinds of things that
are problematic, right?

00:46:15.270 --> 00:46:17.340
Or you can see a problem
where there's not a problem,

00:46:17.340 --> 00:46:21.060
so, it's important for us
to provide the training

00:46:21.060 --> 00:46:24.633
and the awareness so we
can have understanding.

00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:27.480
- Also encouraging, I know
I've said this a few times

00:46:27.480 --> 00:46:29.970
with the APAs, definitely in good hands.

00:46:29.970 --> 00:46:31.380
- Oh, thank you so much,

00:46:31.380 --> 00:46:34.470
I'm grateful for the opportunity to serve

00:46:34.470 --> 00:46:36.540
and really blessed to be able to serve

00:46:36.540 --> 00:46:40.440
with a number of really incredible people

00:46:40.440 --> 00:46:42.900
and glad that you are
in the field as well.

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:45.420
- Yeah, I'm definitely grateful as well.

00:46:45.420 --> 00:46:47.460
Well, this has been a
really rich conversation,

00:46:47.460 --> 00:46:49.830
went too fast, so many things
we could have touched on,

00:46:49.830 --> 00:46:52.770
but any last pieces that you
wanna leave the listeners with

00:46:52.770 --> 00:46:55.380
in terms of what gives you
hope on a day-to-day basis?

00:46:55.380 --> 00:46:57.810
- Yes, so I wanna say one of the things

00:46:57.810 --> 00:47:02.250
that gives me hope is the
arts, artistic expression,

00:47:02.250 --> 00:47:06.370
and so for us to think about
ways to both express ourselves

00:47:07.380 --> 00:47:09.570
through the arts, but also to engage

00:47:09.570 --> 00:47:12.360
with other people's artistry,
so that can be in music,

00:47:12.360 --> 00:47:14.370
I tell people, think of a theme song,

00:47:14.370 --> 00:47:16.830
play it in the morning
before you start working

00:47:16.830 --> 00:47:20.490
or engaging with your
family, moving our bodies

00:47:20.490 --> 00:47:24.270
or dancing, poetry, and
then some people will say,

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:26.700
I'm not artsy, but it
might be culinary arts,

00:47:26.700 --> 00:47:29.220
the way you cook, the
way you wear your hair,

00:47:29.220 --> 00:47:32.370
the way you decorate
your office or your home,

00:47:32.370 --> 00:47:35.850
so that creativity, I think it helps us

00:47:35.850 --> 00:47:39.540
to think out of the box,
which is always a good thing

00:47:39.540 --> 00:47:42.870
and then the last thing I would just say

00:47:42.870 --> 00:47:45.180
is to remember your own care,

00:47:45.180 --> 00:47:47.916
often we are in service of other people,

00:47:47.916 --> 00:47:51.990
but we don't wanna erase,
abandon, or neglect ourselves

00:47:51.990 --> 00:47:55.443
and so I hope you will do
something for you on today.

00:47:56.460 --> 00:47:57.660
- Well thank you so much

00:47:59.883 --> 00:48:02.446
for sharing that with our
listeners, words of wisdom,

00:48:02.446 --> 00:48:04.044
and for any who want to know more

00:48:04.044 --> 00:48:05.877
or hear more about some of the things

00:48:05.877 --> 00:48:07.964
that Dr. Thema has mentioned,

00:48:07.964 --> 00:48:08.969
you can definitely
check out for TED Talks,

00:48:08.969 --> 00:48:10.527
there's lots of rich
insights there as well.

00:48:10.527 --> 00:48:11.954
So again, thank you so much for being here

00:48:11.954 --> 00:48:12.870
on the Addy Hour podcast,
just for your work

00:48:12.870 --> 00:48:14.190
and for all that you came to share

00:48:14.190 --> 00:48:16.590
with our listeners as well
today definitely appreciate it.

00:48:16.590 --> 00:48:18.193
- Oh, thank you so much,

00:48:18.193 --> 00:48:19.950
I appreciate you having me.

